// Long-context suite: conversations long enough to exercise the budget planner.
//
// Opt-in (`--suite long_context`) so these do not move the aggregate metrics
// that `core` baselines were recorded against.
//
// Two classes, testing opposite failure modes:
//
//   history-retention  A fact stated early, then enough filler to be tempting
//                      to trim, but still inside the window. Answering needs
//                      that early turn -- so a failure means we trimmed context
//                      that fit, i.e. threw away good context.
//
//   eviction           Far more history than the window holds, with the needed
//                      fact in the LAST turn before the question. Answering
//                      proves eviction protected the recent end of the
//                      conversation. A failure here is the serious one: it means
//                      the model is losing the thread of what was just said.
//
// Both are graded on arithmetic or a specific figure rather than prose, so a
// pass cannot be scored by a model that merely sounds confident.
{"id": "longctx-early-fact-survives-01", "query": "Given my group size, how many gallons of water should I store for two weeks? Show the arithmetic.", "turns": [{"role": "user", "content": "Before we start: my group is exactly 6 people, and we plan for a 14-day window."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Understood \u2014 6 people over a 14-day window. I'll keep that in mind."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Can you give me a general overview of prepping priorities? (part 1)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The usual ordering is water, then shelter and warmth, then food, then medical, then communications and power. Water comes first because dehydration incapacitates a person in days while a person can go weeks without food."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What about sanitation? (part 2)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Sanitation ranks alongside water in a sustained event. Human waste must be kept well away from any water source and from living areas, and hand hygiene matters more than most people expect once medical care is limited."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How do people usually get this wrong? (part 3)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The most common mistake is buying equipment before building routine. Stored supplies that are never rotated expire, and gear that is never practised with tends not to work when it is needed."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Any advice on training? (part 4)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Practise on ordinary weekends rather than waiting for an emergency. Cook a meal on the camp stove, filter water from a real source, and navigate a route on paper before relying on any of it."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Can you give me a general overview of prepping priorities? (part 5)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The usual ordering is water, then shelter and warmth, then food, then medical, then communications and power. Water comes first because dehydration incapacitates a person in days while a person can go week