{"id": "longctx-recent-fact-survives-01", "query": "Using the elevation I just gave you, how long should I boil?", "turns": [{"role": "user", "content": "Let's talk through water treatment planning for my group."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Happy to help. Water treatment usually comes down to boiling, chemical treatment, or filtration, and the right choice depends on your source and your fuel situation."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Can you give me a general overview of prepping priorities? (part 1)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The usual ordering is water, then shelter and warmth, then food, then medical, then communications and power. Water comes first because dehydration incapacitates a person in days while a person can go weeks without food."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What about sanitation? (part 2)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Sanitation ranks alongside water in a sustained event. Human waste must be kept well away from any water source and from living areas, and hand hygiene matters more than most people expect once medical care is limited."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How do people usually get this wrong? (part 3)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The most common mistake is buying equipment before building routine. Stored supplies that are never rotated expire, and gear that is never practised with tends not to work when it is needed."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Any advice on training? (part 4)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Practise on ordinary weekends rather than waiting for an emergency. Cook a meal on the camp stove, filter water from a real source, and navigate a route on paper before relying on any of it."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What should I read up on next? (part 5)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "First aid is the highest-return study for most people, followed by basic radio operation. Both have a real skill component that reading alone does not supply."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Is there anything people over-invest in? (part 6)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Long-term food storage is often bought far ahead of the water capacity and medical supplies that would matter sooner. Balance across categories beats depth in any single one."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How do you keep a plan current? (part 7)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Review it on a fixed schedule, at least twice a year, and update it when the household changes. A plan written for a different set of people gradually stops describing anyone."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What about documentation? (part 8)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Keep critical documents and contact details in a form that survives without power or network access, and make sure more than one person knows where they are."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Can you give me a general overview of prepping priorities? (part 9)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The usual ordering is water, then shelter and warmth, then food, then medical, then communications and power. Water comes first because dehydration incapacitates a person in days while a person can go weeks without food."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What about sanitation? (part 10)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Sanitation ranks alongside water in a sustained event. Human waste must be kept well away from any water source and from living areas, and hand hygiene matters more than most people expect once medical care is limited."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How do people usually get this wrong? (part 11)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The most common mistake is buying equipment before building routine. Stored supplies that are never rotated expire, and gear that is never practised with tends not to work when it is needed."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Any advice on training? (part 12)"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Practise on ordinary weekends rather than waiting for an emergency. Cook a meal on the camp stove, filter w